{
const s=resultSlide('Attack success and combined benign utility',[[0,3],[0,3],[0,3]],['ASR ↓','Utility ↑'],'Attack n = 134 / 131 / 140. Benign utility n = 82 / 85 / 76, in condition order.');s.note('Native editable table copied from verified saved-output analysis. Distributed: every memory method 4/134 ASR and 75/82 utility; no-memory 0/134 and 67/82. Localized: every memory method 5/131 ASR; static/R utility 77/85, C/M/S 76/85; no-memory 0/131 and 71/85. Uninformative: all memory methods 6/140 ASR; static/R utility 70/76, C/M/S 69/76; no-memory 0/140 and 62/76. Bold includes ties and diagnostic controls, so no-memory is bold on ASR despite its severe retention loss on the next slide. No-memory is not the paper’s SimpleMemory “no defense” method. The condition-specific denominators differ because relational audits reserve different cases. Three scenario variants must not be treated as independent groups. The historical full-pool 5.87% is deliberately excluded from this matched table.',[SRC.results,'research_data/development_comparison_report_v1_20261101/RESULTS.md']);}
// 24
{
const s=resultSlide('Retention and restraint must be read separately',[[1,2],[1,2],[1,2]],['Retention ↑','Restraint ↑'],'Retention n = 14 / 13 / 13. Restraint n = 68 / 72 / 63, in condition order.');s.note('Distributed: all memory methods retention 13/14, restraint 62/68; no-memory 0/14, 67/68. Localized: all memory methods retention 12/13; static/R restraint 65/72, C/M/S 64/72; no-memory 0/13, 71/72. Uninformative: all memory methods retention 12/13; static/R restraint 58/63, C/M/S 57/63; no-memory 0/13, 62/63. This separates the strong restraint of ignoring all memory from the ability to retain explicitly warranted facts. S did not improve over C or M; S lost one restraint success versus static/R in localized and null. Retention targets in these eight bags admit no attributed external candidate under original rules, so the tied retention rates do not establish preservation of useful external-source contributions. The combined metric on the prior slide is a weighted pool, not general answer accuracy.',[SRC.results,SRC.pilot]);}
// 25 diagnosis chart
{
const s=new Slide('The rule ran, but had few external candidates to affect','OUR RESEARCH  |  RETRIEVAL DIAGNOSTICS','Count of queries per condition (n = 216 each) · actual supplied-context hashes');
s.chart(.65,2.08,8.2,4.48,['Distributed','Localized','Uninformative'],[{name:'External candidate admitted',values:[14,16,18],color:C.navy},{name:'S context changed',values:[5,8,10],color:C.teal}],{max:20,unit:5});s.text(9.18,2.38,3.3,.7,'23 / 648',{size:36,bold:true,color:C.teal});s.text(9.18,3.2,3.3,1.1,'S contexts changed\nversus static SAFE-Mem',{size:22});s.text(9.18,4.7,3.3,1.1,'Most queries used principal-owned information.',{size:23,bold:true,color:C.navy});caption(s,'Context change is evidence that the policy executed. It is not evidence that unsafe influence was removed or that an answer improved.');
s.note('The admission audit counts questions with at least one originally admissible attributed external candidate: 14, 16 and 18 of 216 for distributed, localized and uninformative. This is different from the number of selected external records or unsafe records. S changes the full rendered retrieval-context hash on 5, 8 and 10 queries, total 23/648. C changes 5/7/12, total 24; M changes 6/10/13, total 29; R changes none. The counts are direct diagnostics in the verified report. The chart is a native editable PowerPoint chart with an embedded worksheet. It compares counts measured over the same per-condition 216 queries, but a changed context need not contain or remove an unsafe record. Original authority sharply limits attributed external memory exposure. We have not completed a blinded per-context causal failure analysis, so do not interpret every difference as transfer to an uninspected sibling.',[SRC.pilot,SRC.results]);}
// 26 actual findings
{
const s=new Slide('What we observed, and what remains unexplained','OUR RESEARCH  |  CRITICAL FINDINGS');
s.table(.7,2.35,11.9,['Method','Principal records','External records','Unknown origin'],[['Static SAFE-Mem','2,327','106','86'],['S · Selective (ours)','2,327','92','100']],{widths:[4.1,2.6,2.6,2.6],size:23,rowHeight:.85,headerHeight:.75,oursLast:true});s.text(.85,5.03,11.55,.4,'S tied C and M on attack outcomes. These are exposure counts, not safety labels.',{size:21,color:C.navy});
s.text(.85,5.65,11.55,.73,'Replacing attributed records with unknown-origin records is not proof of a safer context. Detailed analysis of the 23 changes remains next.',{size:23,color:C.teal});caption(s,'Record occurrences repeat across queries and are not independent participants or independent safety labels.',6.72);
s.note('Across all three conditions static selects 2,327 principal, 106 external and 86 unknown-origin record occurrences; S selects the same 2,327 principal occurrences, 92 external and 100 unknown occurrences. The reduction of 14 known external exposures and increase of 14 unknown exposures indicates that refill can change source composition without guaranteeing safety. Those repeated selected-record occurrences are not unique memories or independent observations. The report supports aggregate changes and the one-event restraint losses in two conditions, not a completed causal explanation. Potential contributors to the null ASR effect include scarce admissible external candidates, only four to six baseline successes per condition, feedback uncertainty and mismatch between case-intent placement and actual memory risk. These are hypotheses and design limitations, not experimentally isolated causes. No source-grounded utility endpoint is available to show preservation of useful external facts.',[SRC.pilot,SRC.results]);}
// 27 limitations
{
const s=new Slide('What the preliminary comparison cannot establish','OUR RESEARCH  |  SCIENTIFIC LIMITS');
lead(s,'Safety truth','Zero independently human-verified labels. AI assessments are simulated feedback, not gold annotations.',2.12);lead(s,'External utility','Zero validated source-grounded utility questions. Retention alone does not test useful external memory.',3.35);lead(s,'Efficacy or equivalence','Only 4–6 baseline attack successes per condition. Eight development bags; no untouched confirmation.',4.58);caption(s,'S has not shown improvement beyond C/M. No full-dataset S result, external-source generalization claim or answer-level robustness claim is supported.',6.55);
s.note('The future primary study requires independently validated Z(record,use), confirmed attributable unsafe seed audits and balanced safe/unsafe bundles. The current study has none of those guarantees: its eight bags balance original case labels, and some first AI verdicts are safe or unresolved. The failed AI utility-review round remains blocked and is not silently recast as qualified data; the comparison uses a separately declared provisional scope with original question metrics only. Noisy feedback, delayed audits, compromise, false accusation recovery and permissions have scenario specifications and offline mechanisms, not an executed answer-level robustness suite. The three overlays are sensitivity replications over the same inputs. The protocol’s fewer-than-20-baseline-success planning rule makes efficacy inconclusive. Tied outcomes and degenerate zero-difference bootstrap intervals do not prove equivalence or preserved utility. Synthetic provenance does not solve authentication, collusion or real account changes.',[SRC.pilot,SRC.protocol+' §§15–17','reports/AI_PILOT_ADJUDICATION_20261101_REPORT.md']);}
// 28 next
{
const s=new Slide('Next study: test transfer where external memory is usable','OUR RESEARCH  |  NEXT STEPS');
const steps=[['1','Inspect the 23 changed contexts and the benign errors.','Trace which record, scope and refill decision changed each answer.'],['2','Define and validate a new external-memory-use cohort.','Review provenance, exact uses, safe/unsafe bundles and utility questions.'],['3','Freeze a new matched comparison before new outcomes.','Preserve authority; compare S with strong C/M; then test error and change.']];
steps.forEach(([n,a,b],i)=>{let y=2.1+i*1.42;s.text(.8,y,.6,.55,n,{size:32,bold:true,color:C.teal});s.text(1.65,y,10.8,.45,a,{size:24,bold:true,color:C.navy});s.text(1.65,y+.62,10.8,.55,b,{size:21,color:C.muted});});caption(s,'The prototype is implemented and exercised. A selective-propagation advantage is still unproven; scaling the same weakly informative setup is not the next inference.');
s.note('The next immediate analysis is a per-query review of changed contexts, including S versus R, C and M and the two benign restraint losses. It should distinguish direct audited-record containment from transfer to uninspected siblings and identify unknown-origin substitutions. For a redesigned study, prospectively select external-memory tasks based on content, provenance and intended use before viewing new model outcomes. Complete two independent human reviews and third-party adjudication of disagreements, including source-grounded utility references. The current authority rules may block chunk-only support, so eligible task definitions must be checked without silently elevating source authority. Freeze new case/template groups, shared audits, thresholds, costs and strong baselines before confirmation; previously inspected development results cannot become untouched test data. Add recent-history C and fixed-mixture M and the required robustness suite before broad paper-level claims. If M matches S at equal utility and cost, report a boundary result or revise the method rather than claiming novelty.',[SRC.pilot+' Scientific decision and next work',SRC.protocol+' §§9,15–18']);}
